---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";

const props = {
	title: "Arabic OCR/HWR with Qwen2",
	tagline:
		"Finetuning Qwen2 on Arabic text lines with synthetic + collected data, the model-side track of Makhtotah.",
	description:
		"Qwen2 finetuning experiments for Arabic OCR and handwritten-text recognition.",
	status: "ongoing" as const,
	startDate: "2025",
	type: "research" as const,
	role: "Researcher, lead implementer",
	team: "Sub-track of Makhtotah, advised by Dr. Omar Alomair",
	stack: ["Python", "PyTorch", "Qwen2", "HuggingFace"],
	relatedProjects: [
		{ slug: "makhtotah-research", title: "Makhtotah Research" },
		{ slug: "makhtotah-platform", title: "Makhtotah Platform" },
	],
	toc: [
		{ id: "why", label: "Why this experiment" },
		{ id: "data", label: "Data" },
		{ id: "what-i-found", label: "What I found" },
	],
};
---

<ProjectLayout {...props}>
	<h2 id="why">Why this experiment</h2>
	<p>
		The Makhtotah Research project showed that for degraded Arabic
		manuscripts, preprocessing wins are larger than model wins. But for
		modern Arabic (printed and handwritten lines) that conclusion
		flips: a strong general-purpose recognizer plus light adaptation can
		out-perform a model trained from scratch. Qwen2 was the obvious place
		to test that.
	</p>

	<h2 id="data">Data</h2>
	<p>
		Two sources, intentionally:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Collected.</strong> Real Arabic text lines pulled from public datasets and clean scans, with line-level transcription.</li>
		<li><strong>Synthetic.</strong> Programmatically rendered lines covering the long tail of glyphs the collected set under-represents: unusual ligatures, rare characters, diacritic combinations.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Synthetic data is great for <em>character coverage</em> and mediocre
		for <em>degradation patterns</em>. So I use it to fix the
		distribution, not to substitute for real images.
	</p>

	<h2 id="what-i-found">What I found</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Light finetuning on Qwen2 closes the gap to specialized OCR architectures on clean text.</li>
		<li>HWR (handwritten) is much harder; collected handwriting samples dominate, synthetic helps less here.</li>
		<li>Decoding strategy matters more than I expected: beam search with character-level constraints reduces error on near-miss confusions (similar shapes, missing dots).</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		The work continues to feed into Makhtotah Research and the
		Platform's recognition layer.
	</p>
</ProjectLayout>
